#
# prima-materia — Tiantai, taken down from the register at the wording of the
# Xiao zhiguan
#
# The examinability-driven entrance of SPEC §14, which has priority. pmt:Tiantai
# stood in traditions/registry-buddhist.ttl on pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed
# with zero nodes, and its registration already named what this entrance looks
# for: a systematic account of the obstacles, illnesses and demonic states that
# arise in practice. The reading bears the registration out and goes past it.
# This work does four things no opened work of this holding has done together:
# it names bodily signs by which its own mis-performance is recognised before
# anything else goes wrong; it names madness and death as consequences of its
# own procedure wrongly used; it sets out the counterfeit of its own signs of
# success beside the signs themselves, with a criterion; and it closes the one
# procedure it states a guarantee for with two clauses that make the guarantee
# unanswerable.
#
# Why this work and not the Mohe zhiguan. The registration names both. The
# Xiao zhiguan is the short one, it is the author's own summary of the long one
# for a lay reader, and it is short enough to be read through in one run, which
# is what the harvest rule asks for and what the Mohe zhiguan in twenty juan
# would not have allowed. It stands here as pm:corpusHarvested, the first node
# of this holding to carry that state: the work was read from the preface to
# the last line, and what the state claims is completeness for the work opened
# and nothing about the corpus. The Mohe zhiguan stands as pm:corpusWork and is
# unopened, so queries/unopened-works.rq carries it.
#
# On the citations. The canonical scheme is the Taishō reference, volume,
# number, page and register, which the CBETA edition of that text carries as
# line beacons and which a reader finds in any printing of the canon. It is
# given as T46.1915.<page><register>. The register letter is part of the place
# and not an addition: a Taishō page holds three of them.
#
# Order of the file: the tradition and its works, the practices, the capacities,
# the prerequisites with their stated consequences, the conditions of failure,
# the signs of success kept separate from them, and last the three clauses that
# close the account.
#

pmt:Tiantai a pm:Tradition ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:XiaoZhiguan ;
    rdfs:label "Tiantai"@en, "Tiantai"@de ;
    skos:definition "The Chinese transmission of calming and contemplation, with a systematic account of the obstacles, illnesses and demonic states that arise in practice."@en ;
    skos:note "Read in this run at the wording, from the preface of Yuanzhao of 1095 to the last line: Zhiyi, Xiuxi zhiguan zuochan fayao, also called Tongmeng zhiguan and Xiao zhiguan, T46 no. 1915, in the Taishō text as the CBETA edition carries it. All ten chapters were read: the five outward conditions, the reproach of the five desires, the discarding of the five coverings, the regulating of the five matters, the five expedients, the main practice, the arising of the good roots, the recognition of demonic affairs, the curing of illness and the final realization. Nothing of this work was left unread."@en ;
    skos:note "The English of Lu Kuan Yu (Charles Luk), The Secrets of Chinese Meditation, London: Rider, 1964, chapter 4, was read beside the Chinese and used as a control on the clauses this file turns on. It is one translator and not two: the two-translation requirement of SPEC §15 is not met for this work and the reason is given at the search node in examinations/searches-tiantai.ttl."@en ;
    skos:note "The contact route stays at documented and this run did not change it. The work quotes the Lotus Sūtra, the Mahāprajñāpāramitā treatise, the Daśabhūmika, the Awakening of Faith and a Miaosheng ding jing by name, and takes the four breath marks and the five good roots from the meditation literature it inherits; nothing here is proposed as an independent witness. What the reading did establish is that the three clauses at the end of the healing chapter are this work speaking in its own voice and not a quotation, because the quotations in it are marked and these are not."@en ;
    skos:note "What kind of witness this work is. It is a manual written by the founder of the school for his lay elder brother, and it reads as instructions to a named beginner: it says what to do, what will go wrong, what that will feel like in the body, and what to do then. That form is the reason it carries what this holding is short of. It also means it reports no case: in the whole work there is not one instance of a person who did these things and what came of it."@en ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Zhiyi, Mohe zhiguan; Zhiyi, Xiao zhiguan" ;
    pm:corpusWork pmw:XiaoZhiguan, pmw:MoheZhiguan ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusHarvested ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    dcterms:source "Zhiyi, Xiuxi zhiguan zuochan fayao (Xiao zhiguan), T46.1915.0462b, in Taishō shinshū daizōkyō vol. 46, Tokyo 1924-1934, read in the CBETA edition of that text" .

#
# The works of this corpus as identifiers
#

pmw:XiaoZhiguan a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Xiuxi zhiguan zuochan fayao, the small treatise on calming and contemplation"@en, "Xiuxi zhiguan zuochan fayao, die kleine Abhandlung über Ruhe und Schau"@de ;
    skos:note "Opened and read entire on 2026-10-10 in the Taishō text, T46 no. 1915, pages 462a to 473a, as the CBETA edition of that text carries it. One juan, ten chapters, with the preface Yuanzhao of Yuhang wrote for the printing of 1095. The preface is itself a source for the work's standing: it names the four treatises on calming and contemplation of the Tiantai master and says this is the fourth of them, drawn up for the master's lay elder brother Chen Zhen as the outline of the large one."@en .

pmw:MoheZhiguan a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Mohe zhiguan, the great treatise on calming and contemplation"@en, "Mohe zhiguan, die große Abhandlung über Ruhe und Schau"@de ;
    skos:note "Named first by the registration and unopened. The reason is the size and not the access: the text is reachable in the same edition as the work opened here, at T46 no. 1911 in twenty juan, and reading it is a run of its own. The Xiao zhiguan was taken first because it is the author's own summary of it and could be read through."@en .

#
# The practices
#

pmp:TiantaiZhiguan a pm:Practicing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:XiaoZhiguan ;
    rdfs:label "Calming and contemplation in seated practice"@en, "Ruhe und Schau im Sitzen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The paired procedure the work teaches. Calming is the first gate for subduing the bonds, contemplation the main instrument for cutting delusion; the work calls them the two wheels of a cart and the two wings of a bird. Seated, the practitioner takes the half or full cross-legged posture, loosens the belt, lays the left hand on the right, straightens the spine so that nose and navel face each other, expels the stale breath three times through the open mouth, closes the mouth with the tongue to the palate and the eyes just enough to cut the outer light, and sits as still as a set stone. Within one sitting the three matters of body, breath and mind are watched and corrected as each goes out of adjustment; five turns of calming and contemplation are then applied according to which fault appears, and the same five are carried into walking, standing, sitting, lying, working and speaking, and into each of the six senses."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Tiantai ;
    dcterms:source "Zhiyi, Xiuxi zhiguan zuochan fayao (Xiao zhiguan), T46.1915.0465c to 0469a, in Taishō shinshū daizōkyō vol. 46, Tokyo 1924-1934, read in the CBETA edition of that text" .

pmp:TiantaiTiaoxi a pm:Practicing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:XiaoZhiguan ;
    rdfs:label "Regulating the breath by its four marks"@en, "Die Atemregelung nach ihren vier Merkmalen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The breath has four marks and three of them are marks of maladjustment. Wind is when the breath is audible at the nostrils in sitting. Panting is when it is silent but knotted and does not pass. Air is when it is neither audible nor knotted but not fine. Breath proper is when it is neither audible nor knotted nor coarse, passing continuously as if present and as if absent. Where one of the first three is found, three measures are prescribed: settle the mind downward, loosen the body, and think the air passing in and out through all the pores without obstruction."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Tiantai ;
    dcterms:source "Zhiyi, Xiuxi zhiguan zuochan fayao (Xiao zhiguan), T46.1915.0466a, in Taishō shinshū daizōkyō vol. 46, Tokyo 1924-1934, read in the CBETA edition of that text" .

pmp:TiantaiChuding a pm:Practicing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:XiaoZhiguan ;
    rdfs:label "The procedure for coming out of the sitting"@en, "Das Verfahren für das Herauskommen aus der Sitzung"@de ;
    skos:definition "Before leaving the seat the mind is first released to another object, the mouth opened and the air let go with the thought of it dispersing from all the vessels at will; then the body is moved a little, then the shoulders, arms, hands, head and neck, then both feet until they are soft; then the pores are rubbed over with the hands, the hands rubbed warm and laid over both eyes, and the eyes opened after that. The seat is left only when the heat of the body has subsided."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Tiantai ;
    dcterms:source "Zhiyi, Xiuxi zhiguan zuochan fayao (Xiao zhiguan), T46.1915.0466b, in Taishō shinshū daizōkyō vol. 46, Tokyo 1924-1934, read in the CBETA edition of that text" .

pmp:TiantaiChanhui a pm:Practicing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:XiaoZhiguan ;
    rdfs:label "The repentance that clears the obstruction of broken precepts"@en, "Die Reue, die das Hindernis gebrochener Gelöbnisse beseitigt"@de ;
    skos:definition "The procedure prescribed for the third kind of practitioner, the one who has broken precepts heavy and light. Ten things assist it: clear belief in cause and effect, the arising of grave fear, deep shame, the seeking of a method of extinction, the disclosure of the earlier offence, the cutting of the continuing mind, the raising of a mind that protects the teaching, the making of a great vow to deliver living beings, the constant recollection of the buddhas of the ten directions, and the contemplation of the offence as unborn. The place of practice is adorned, the body washed, clean clothes put on, incense burned and flowers scattered, and the confession carried on single-mindedly before the three jewels for one or three weeks, or a month, or three months, or up to a year, and it stops when extinction is reached."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Tiantai ;
    dcterms:source "Zhiyi, Xiuxi zhiguan zuochan fayao (Xiao zhiguan), T46.1915.0462c to 0463a, in Taishō shinshū daizōkyō vol. 46, Tokyo 1924-1934, read in the CBETA edition of that text" .

pmp:TiantaiShierXi a pm:Practicing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:XiaoZhiguan ;
    rdfs:label "The twelve breaths worked by visualization against named complaints"@en, "Die zwölf Atemarten, durch Vorstellung gegen benannte Leiden geführt"@de ;
    skos:definition "Twelve breaths, all of them produced from the visualizing mind, each assigned to one complaint: the upward breath treats sinking heaviness, the downward breath empty suspension, the full breath wasting, the scorching breath swelling, the growing breath debility, the destroying breath excess, the warm breath cold, the cold breath heat, the thrusting breath stoppage and blockage, the holding breath trembling, the harmonizing breath the general disharmony of the four elements, and the supplementing breath their decline. Beside them the work gives six sounded breaths matched to the organs in a verse: he to the heart, chui to the kidneys, hu to the spleen, si to the lungs, xu when heat comes from the liver, and xi where the triple burner is congested."@en ;
    skos:note "The work does not teach these in its own voice. Each set is introduced with a master says, the masters are unnamed, and four such opinions on healing by calming and three on healing by contemplation are set side by side without the work deciding between them. What the work adds in its own voice is the withdrawal entered at pmc:TiantaiJinshiBuchengjiu."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Tiantai ;
    dcterms:source "Zhiyi, Xiuxi zhiguan zuochan fayao (Xiao zhiguan), T46.1915.0471c to 0472a, in Taishō shinshū daizōkyō vol. 46, Tokyo 1924-1934, read in the CBETA edition of that text" .

#
# The capacities
#

pmc:TiantaiSibaisibing a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:XiaoZhiguan ;
    rdfs:label "The four hundred and four illnesses are removed of themselves, or brought on"@en, "Die vierhundertvier Krankheiten vergehen von selbst, oder werden hervorgebracht"@de ;
    skos:definition "The claim the healing chapter opens with, stated in both directions in one sentence: as for the method of seated meditation, if a man is able to use the mind well, then the four hundred and four illnesses are removed of themselves; if his use of the mind misses its place, then the four hundred and four illnesses arise from it. The number is the canonical one, a hundred and one for each of the four elements."@en ;
    skos:note "The strongest single claim of the work and the one place where it states its own failure mode in the same breath as its effect, with the same magnitude on both sides. It is answerable in principle on a named person, and the work supplies no sign by which the two directions could be told apart in advance."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Tiantai ;
    dcterms:source "Zhiyi, Xiuxi zhiguan zuochan fayao (Xiao zhiguan), T46.1915.0471b, in Taishō shinshū daizōkyō vol. 46, Tokyo 1924-1934, read in the CBETA edition of that text" .

pmc:TiantaiZhiguanYieldsSibaisibing a pm:Yielding ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:XiaoZhiguan ;
    rdfs:label "Seated calming and contemplation removes the four hundred and four illnesses"@en, "Ruhe und Schau im Sitzen beseitigt die vierhundertvier Krankheiten"@de ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:TiantaiZhiguan ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:TiantaiSibaisibing ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Tiantai ;
    dcterms:source "Zhiyi, Xiuxi zhiguan zuochan fayao (Xiao zhiguan), T46.1915.0471b, in Taishō shinshū daizōkyō vol. 46, Tokyo 1924-1934, read in the CBETA edition of that text" .

pmc:TiantaiShierXiZhibing a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:XiaoZhiguan ;
    rdfs:label "Each of the twelve breaths treats the complaint assigned to it"@en, "Jede der zwölf Atemarten behandelt das ihr zugewiesene Leiden"@de ;
    skos:definition "The capacity ascribed to the twelve breaths, with the pairing complete as the work gives it: upward against sinking heaviness, downward against empty suspension, full against wasting, scorching against swelling, growing against debility, destroying against excess, warm against cold, cold against heat, thrusting against stoppage, holding against trembling, harmonizing against the disharmony of the four elements, supplementing against their decline. Used well, the work says of them, these breaths can treat all complaints throughout."@en ;
    skos:note "Twelve prescriptions, each with its indication, and the harvest rule of SPEC §15 counts a prescription as a capacity and asks for strict completeness. They are entered here as one capacity with the twelve pairings complete rather than as twelve nodes, and the ground is not economy: the work does not claim them. It reports them as the opinion of an unnamed master among several it sets side by side, and three lines later it withdraws them for its own time. Twelve nodes would have recorded twelve claims of this transmission where the transmission makes none. The alternative was available and is named here so that a later reader can take it: the pairings are in the definition and nothing has to be read again to split them."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Tiantai ;
    dcterms:source "Zhiyi, Xiuxi zhiguan zuochan fayao (Xiao zhiguan), T46.1915.0472a, in Taishō shinshū daizōkyō vol. 46, Tokyo 1924-1934, read in the CBETA edition of that text" .

pmc:TiantaiShierXiYieldsZhibing a pm:Yielding ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:XiaoZhiguan ;
    rdfs:label "The twelve breaths are reported to treat the twelve complaints"@en, "Von den zwölf Atemarten wird berichtet, sie behandelten die zwölf Leiden"@de ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:TiantaiShierXi ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:TiantaiShierXiZhibing ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Tiantai ;
    skos:note "Third-party ascription and not textual attestation, because the work attributes the set to an unnamed master and does not take it up. The mode is the whole finding about these twelve: a manual that states its own failure conditions elsewhere in its own voice declines to own the one set of prescriptions in it that reads like a pharmacopoeia."@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zhiyi, Xiuxi zhiguan zuochan fayao (Xiao zhiguan), T46.1915.0472a, in Taishō shinshū daizōkyō vol. 46, Tokyo 1924-1934, read in the CBETA edition of that text" .

pmc:TiantaiShentong a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:XiaoZhiguan ;
    rdfs:label "Attaining the absorption one is able to bring forth the powers of the way"@en, "Mit der Vertiefung vermag man die Kräfte des Weges hervorzubringen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The capacity the chapter on the five expedients holds out as the reason for valuing the absorption above the world: if one gains the absorption, one is then able to bring forth in full the undefiled knowledges, all the transcendent powers and strength of the way, to accomplish the right and equal awakening and to deliver living beings widely. At the end of the work the same thing is said of the completed middle contemplation: the six senses become pure, all the teachings of the buddhas appear before one, the recollection absorption is accomplished and one abides in the Śūraṅgama absorption, which is the absorption of the universal manifestation of bodily form."@en ;
    skos:note "The only capacities in the work that are not healing or quiet are stated here and nowhere else, and both times as a consequence of a stage and never of an operation anyone could repeat. The work gives no sign by which either would be recognised from outside."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Tiantai ;
    dcterms:source "Zhiyi, Xiuxi zhiguan zuochan fayao (Xiao zhiguan), T46.1915.0466c, in Taishō shinshū daizōkyō vol. 46, Tokyo 1924-1934, read in the CBETA edition of that text", "Zhiyi, Xiuxi zhiguan zuochan fayao (Xiao zhiguan), T46.1915.0472c, in Taishō shinshū daizōkyō vol. 46, Tokyo 1924-1934, read in the CBETA edition of that text" .

pmc:TiantaiZhiguanYieldsShentong a pm:Yielding ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:XiaoZhiguan ;
    rdfs:label "Calming and contemplation brings forth the powers of the way"@en, "Ruhe und Schau bringt die Kräfte des Weges hervor"@de ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:TiantaiZhiguan ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:TiantaiShentong ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Tiantai ;
    dcterms:source "Zhiyi, Xiuxi zhiguan zuochan fayao (Xiao zhiguan), T46.1915.0466c, in Taishō shinshū daizōkyō vol. 46, Tokyo 1924-1934, read in the CBETA edition of that text" .

pmc:TiantaiZhiguanIsWithdrawnAbsorption a pm:Situating ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:XiaoZhiguan ;
    rdfs:label "The seated practice is placed at withdrawn absorption"@en, "Die Sitzpraxis liegt bei abgezogener Vertiefung"@de ;
    pm:situatedNode pmp:TiantaiZhiguan ;
    pm:axisValue pm:withdrawnAbsorption ;
    pm:situationGround "The work prescribes that the eyes be closed just enough to cut off the outer light, that the body be still as a set stone, and that the mind be bound to the nose tip or the navel without a scattered thought, and it treats dullness and sleep as faults to be corrected rather than as the medium of the practice. That is the criterion this coordinate names. The placement is of the seated practice; the same work carries the five turns into walking, working and speaking, and that extension is expressly a practice in the ordinary waking channel and is not placed here."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Tiantai ;
    dcterms:source "Zhiyi, Xiuxi zhiguan zuochan fayao (Xiao zhiguan), T46.1915.0465c, in Taishō shinshū daizōkyō vol. 46, Tokyo 1924-1934, read in the CBETA edition of that text" .

pmc:TiantaiZhiguanIsOwnAgency a pm:Situating ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:XiaoZhiguan ;
    rdfs:label "The seated practice is placed at own agency"@en, "Die Sitzpraxis liegt bei eigener Handlungsträgerschaft"@de ;
    pm:situatedNode pmp:TiantaiZhiguan ;
    pm:axisValue pm:ownAgency ;
    pm:situationGround "Every instruction of the work is addressed to the practitioner as the one who acts: he regulates, he corrects, he retains or drops, and where a power outside him appears it appears as an interference to be dismissed and never as the agent of the result. The chapter on demonic affairs makes the point for the compiler: the work refuses to let even the harm be another's doing."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Tiantai ;
    dcterms:source "Zhiyi, Xiuxi zhiguan zuochan fayao (Xiao zhiguan), T46.1915.0471a, in Taishō shinshū daizōkyō vol. 46, Tokyo 1924-1934, read in the CBETA edition of that text" .

#
# The prerequisites the work states with their consequences
#

pmc:TiantaiWuyuan a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:XiaoZhiguan ;
    rdfs:label "The five outward conditions to be had before the practice begins"@en, "Die fünf äußeren Bedingungen, die vor dem Beginn vorliegen müssen"@de ;
    skos:definition "First the precepts kept pure, with the repentance procedure for the one who has broken them. Second clothing and food sufficient, in one of three measures of clothing and four of food. Third a quiet dwelling, of which three are allowed: deep mountain away from people, an ascetic retreat three or four li from the nearest settlement so that the sound of herding is gone, and a clean monastery away from lay dwellings. Fourth the cutting off of occupations, in four kinds: livelihood, social relations, crafts and skills including medicine, spells, divination and reckoning, and the business of learning, reciting and lecturing. Fifth the nearness of good friends, in three kinds: the outward protector who provides, the fellow practitioner who practises the same way, and the teacher who shows the gates of the practice inward and outward."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Tiantai ;
    dcterms:source "Zhiyi, Xiuxi zhiguan zuochan fayao (Xiao zhiguan), T46.1915.0462c to 0463b, in Taishō shinshū daizōkyō vol. 46, Tokyo 1924-1934, read in the CBETA edition of that text" .

pmc:TiantaiPresupposesYishi a pm:Presupposing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:XiaoZhiguan ;
    rdfs:label "The practice presupposes sufficient clothing and food and freedom from occupations"@en, "Die Praxis setzt hinreichende Kleidung und Nahrung und die Freiheit von Geschäften voraus"@de ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:TiantaiZhiguan ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:TiantaiWuyuan ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "Of the second condition: for what reason? Without these conditions the mind is not at rest, and there is obstruction to the way. Of the fourth: for what reason? If the occupations are many, then the business of practising the way is abandoned, the mind is disordered and hard to gather in. And of too much possession under the second: if one is greedy for more and piles it up, then the mind is disordered and the way is hindered."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Tiantai ;
    skos:note "Stated necessity and not prescribed order, because the work asks its own question after each of the two conditions and answers it with the consequence of the want. The two are entered under one node because the consequence is the same sentence in different words and the prior step is the same list."@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zhiyi, Xiuxi zhiguan zuochan fayao (Xiao zhiguan), T46.1915.0463b, in Taishō shinshū daizōkyō vol. 46, Tokyo 1924-1934, read in the CBETA edition of that text" .

pmc:TiantaiTiaoshi a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:XiaoZhiguan ;
    rdfs:label "Regulating food, with the four ways it goes wrong"@en, "Die Regelung der Nahrung, mit den vier Weisen ihres Fehlgehens"@de ;
    skos:definition "Food is taken to support the body and further the way. If it is too full, the breath is urgent and the body swollen, the hundred vessels do not pass, the mind is shut and the thought in sitting is not at rest. If it is too little, the body is weak and the mind hangs loose and the intention is not firm. Unclean food makes the consciousness dark and confused. Food that does not suit stirs an old illness and sets the four elements against one another. The work calls this the beginning of cultivating the absorption and says it is deeply to be guarded against."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Tiantai ;
    dcterms:source "Zhiyi, Xiuxi zhiguan zuochan fayao (Xiao zhiguan), T46.1915.0465b, in Taishō shinshū daizōkyō vol. 46, Tokyo 1924-1934, read in the CBETA edition of that text" .

pmc:TiantaiPresupposesTiaoshi a pm:Presupposing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:XiaoZhiguan ;
    rdfs:label "The absorption presupposes the measure of food, on pain of a named bodily state"@en, "Die Vertiefung setzt das Maß der Nahrung voraus, bei Strafe eines benannten Körperzustands"@de ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:TiantaiZhiguan ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:TiantaiTiaoshi ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "These two are both not the way to gain the absorption: if the food is too full, the breath is urgent and the body swollen, the hundred vessels do not pass, the mind is shut and the thought in sitting is not at rest; if the food is too little, the body is weak, the mind hangs loose and the intention is not firm. Food that does not suit stirs an old illness and sets the four elements against one another."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Tiantai ;
    skos:note "What makes this clause worth a node is that the consequence is in the body and not in the result. A reader who wants to know whether the practitioner had the condition can ask about the swollen belly and the urgent breath, and the answer does not depend on whether the absorption came."@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zhiyi, Xiuxi zhiguan zuochan fayao (Xiao zhiguan), T46.1915.0465b, in Taishō shinshū daizōkyō vol. 46, Tokyo 1924-1934, read in the CBETA edition of that text" .

pmc:TiantaiPresupposesChuding a pm:Presupposing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:XiaoZhiguan ;
    rdfs:label "Leaving the sitting presupposes the exit procedure, on pain of headache and stiffened joints"@en, "Das Verlassen der Sitzung setzt das Austrittsverfahren voraus, bei Strafe von Kopfschmerz und erstarrten Gelenken"@de ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:TiantaiZhiguan ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:TiantaiChuding ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "If he does not do so, then although he may have gained the abiding mind in the sitting, his coming out being abrupt and hurried, the fine state has not dispersed and remains in the body, and it makes the man headache and the hundred joints of the bones stiff, as it were a wind exhaustion; and in the sittings after it he is vexed and not at rest. Therefore when the mind means to come out of the absorption, attention is needed every time."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Tiantai ;
    skos:note "The clearest falsifiable clause in the work and the one a reader could act on without believing anything else in it. The procedure is short, the omission is a single act, the consequence is named, bodily, and arrives in the same session or the next, and the work states it of every sitting and not of a special case. Nothing in this holding has stood this close to a statement that could be checked by doing the opposite twice."@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zhiyi, Xiuxi zhiguan zuochan fayao (Xiao zhiguan), T46.1915.0466b, in Taishō shinshū daizōkyō vol. 46, Tokyo 1924-1934, read in the CBETA edition of that text" .

pmc:TiantaiPresupposesShanzhishi a pm:Presupposing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:XiaoZhiguan ;
    rdfs:label "The beginner presupposes a teacher at hand, on pain of derangement and death"@en, "Der Anfänger setzt einen Lehrer in der Nähe voraus, bei Strafe von Verwirrung und Tod"@de ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:TiantaiZhiguan ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:TiantaiWuyuan ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "Therefore the practitioner of a beginning mind must keep near a good friend, because there are difficulties of this kind: this is the demon entering a man's mind, and it can make the practitioner's mind and spirit deranged, now glad and now grieving, and through this he falls ill and comes to death. Or it may make him gain false absorptions, false knowledge, powers and spells, so that he preaches and teaches and all believe and submit to him, and afterwards he ruins the good works of other men and destroys the true teaching."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Tiantai ;
    skos:note "The fifth of the five outward conditions is the only one whose want the work costs at the practitioner's life, and the sentence is in the chapter on demonic affairs and not in the chapter of the conditions. The second half is the more interesting one: the work describes a practitioner who acquires persuasive powers and a following, and enters that among the outcomes to be feared rather than among the capacities to be sought."@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zhiyi, Xiuxi zhiguan zuochan fayao (Xiao zhiguan), T46.1915.0471a, in Taishō shinshū daizōkyō vol. 46, Tokyo 1924-1934, read in the CBETA edition of that text" .

#
# The conditions of failure, which is why this work was opened
#

pmc:TiantaiYizhiFakuang a pm:Cautioning ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:XiaoZhiguan ;
    rdfs:label "Forcing the mind to abide often brings on madness"@en, "Das Erzwingen des Verweilens bringt häufig Wahnsinn hervor"@de ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:TiantaiZhiguan ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Tiantai ;
    skos:note "The clause stands immediately after the work has quoted the Awakening of Faith on gathering the scattered mind back into right recollection, and it is the work's own comment on that instruction: for one of a beginning mind in training, abiding is not yet to be had; to press it so that it abides often brings on madness. It is like the method of archery, where one hits the mark after long practice. A self-warning inside the passage prescribing the thing warned against, and the consequence is named."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered as a caution and not as a prerequisite, because the thing to be avoided is not the omission of a step but a degree of force in performing the prescribed step, and the work gives no measure of that degree. The consequence is quotable and is quoted here; pm:Cautioning has no slot for it, which is prima-materia#621."@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zhiyi, Xiuxi zhiguan zuochan fayao (Xiao zhiguan), T46.1915.0467a, in Taishō shinshū daizōkyō vol. 46, Tokyo 1924-1934, read in the CBETA edition of that text" .

pmc:TiantaiYongxinShisuo a pm:Cautioning ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:XiaoZhiguan ;
    rdfs:label "The practice wrongly used brings on the illnesses and may cost the life"@en, "Falsch gebrauchte Praxis bringt die Krankheiten hervor und kann das Leben kosten"@de ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:TiantaiZhiguan ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Tiantai ;
    skos:note "The opening of the healing chapter, and it is addressed to the practitioner about his own operation: as the practitioner settles his mind and cultivates the way, the four elements may have an illness, and because he now uses contemplation the mind and breath beat against it and stir up the underlying illness; or at times he cannot adjust the three matters of body, mind and breath well, and transgresses inwardly or outwardly, and so there is illness. If the mind is used well the four hundred and four illnesses are removed of themselves, and if the use of the mind misses its place the four hundred and four illnesses arise from it. Once an illness is stirred, not only is there obstruction to practising the way, but the great life itself is to be feared for."@en ;
    skos:note "This is the shape SPEC §15 calls a self-warning of a text against what it prescribes, and it is unusually complete: the work names the mechanism, the magnitude, the two directions and the worst outcome, and then devotes the longest chapter of the ten to the remedy. What it does not name is a sign by which the right use could be told from the wrong one before the illness appears."@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zhiyi, Xiuxi zhiguan zuochan fayao (Xiao zhiguan), T46.1915.0471b, in Taishō shinshū daizōkyō vol. 46, Tokyo 1924-1934, read in the CBETA edition of that text" .

pmc:TiantaiPianxiuXieDao a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:XiaoZhiguan ;
    rdfs:label "Practising one of the two alone falls into the false and the upside down"@en, "Nur eines der beiden zu üben fällt in das Falsche und Verkehrte"@de ;
    skos:definition "The condition the work sets on its own pairing, at the head of the whole treatise: these two methods are like the two wheels of a cart and the two wings of a bird, and if they are practised one-sidedly one falls into the false and the upside down. The scripture is quoted for the two outcomes: he who cultivates the absorption and its merit one-sidedly and does not learn wisdom is called stupid, and he who learns wisdom one-sidedly and does not cultivate the absorption and its merit is called mad. The faults of madness and stupidity differ a little, but in the turning round of false views there is no difference at all."@en ;
    skos:note "A condition of failure on the method as a whole, and the one place in the work where the two halves of the method are said to be dangerous apart. It is also the first of five places where this work puts madness at the end of a wrong performance of what it teaches. The other four are the forcing of the abiding mind at pmc:TiantaiYizhiFakuang, the clinging to a counterfeit absorption at pmc:TiantaiXieweiChan, the fright or the clinging at a demonic show at pmc:TiantaiFeiMoSuoWei, and the beginner without a teacher at pmc:TiantaiPresupposesShanzhishi, where the derangement ends in death. Beside them stands a sixth use that is not of this kind: the five hundred immortals of the Snow Mountains who heard the kiṃnara woman sing, lost their absorption and were drunk and mad, which is an exemplum from outside the practice and is not entered."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Tiantai ;
    dcterms:source "Zhiyi, Xiuxi zhiguan zuochan fayao (Xiao zhiguan), T46.1915.0462b, in Taishō shinshū daizōkyō vol. 46, Tokyo 1924-1934, read in the CBETA edition of that text" .

pmc:TiantaiShouFeng a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:XiaoZhiguan ;
    rdfs:label "Guarding wind scatters, guarding panting knots, guarding air exhausts"@en, "Wind bewahren zerstreut, Keuchen bewahren verknotet, Luft bewahren erschöpft"@de ;
    skos:definition "The four outcomes the work attaches to the four breath marks, one line each: to guard the wind is to scatter, to guard the panting is to knot, to guard the air is to exhaust, to guard the breath is to be settled. Where the first three marks are present in sitting it is called unadjusted, and if the practitioner then uses his mind on it, it becomes a trouble of the mind as well and the mind too is hard to settle. The mark of a regulated breath is given as a rule a practitioner can apply to himself: neither rough nor slippery."@en ;
    skos:note "The most answerable thing in the work after the exit procedure, and of a different kind: it is a sign available before the operation has an outcome. The practitioner is told to listen for a sound at his own nostrils and to feel whether the breath knots, and the work predicts what follows from sitting on with each of the three. Three predictions, each with an antecedent anybody present could also check."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Tiantai ;
    dcterms:source "Zhiyi, Xiuxi zhiguan zuochan fayao (Xiao zhiguan), T46.1915.0466a, in Taishō shinshū daizōkyō vol. 46, Tokyo 1924-1934, read in the CBETA edition of that text" .

pmc:TiantaiJiKuanBing a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:XiaoZhiguan ;
    rdfs:label "The disease of the tight mind and the disease of the loose mind, each by its bodily sign"@en, "Die Krankheit des straffen und die des lockeren Geistes, je an ihrem Körperzeichen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Two faults of regulating the mind, each named with the sign by which it is known and the correction. The mark of the disease of a tight mind: from gathering the mind and applying recollection in the sitting, and entering the absorption by it, there is therefore an urgent pain upward in the chest and breast; the mind is then to be loosened and the air thought to flow downward, and the trouble heals of itself. The mark of the disease of a loose mind: the mind feels slack and scattered, the body inclines to sag, or saliva runs from the mouth, or at times there is darkness; the body is then to be drawn in and the recollection made urgent so that the mind abides in its object. Beside them stand the mark of sinking, a dark mind with the head hanging low, and the mark of floating, a drifting mind with the body not at rest."@en ;
    skos:note "Four misadjustments, each with an outward sign, and two of the four signs are visible to a bystander: the head hanging low and the saliva at the mouth. In a holding whose usual trouble is that only the operator can say what happened, a transmission that names a sign a third person can see is the thing to keep, and this work names two without being asked for any."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Tiantai ;
    dcterms:source "Zhiyi, Xiuxi zhiguan zuochan fayao (Xiao zhiguan), T46.1915.0466a, in Taishō shinshū daizōkyō vol. 46, Tokyo 1924-1934, read in the CBETA edition of that text" .

pmc:TiantaiBingJiuNanYu a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:XiaoZhiguan ;
    rdfs:label "Treated at once the illness is easily cured, left long it is hard to heal"@en, "Sogleich behandelt ist die Krankheit leicht zu heilen, lange gelassen schwer"@de ;
    skos:definition "The term the work sets on its own remedy: illnesses of this kind, treated as soon as they are got, are very easily cured; if a long time passes the illness is formed, the body is weak and the illness knotted, and it is hard to heal by treatment. Three causes of illness are distinguished before the term is stated and the remedy differs by cause: the increase and decrease of the four elements and the five organs, illness made by ghosts and spirits, which calls for a strong mind with spells to assist, and illness as the recompense of deeds, which requires the cultivation of merit and repentance."@en ;
    skos:note "A term with a direction, which is rarer in this holding than a term with a date. It does not say when the remedy stops working, only that the chance of it falls as the illness ages, and that is enough to make a late failure expected rather than a counter-instance. The three causes do a second thing of the same kind: two of them take the case out of the reach of the breath and visualization altogether."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Tiantai ;
    dcterms:source "Zhiyi, Xiuxi zhiguan zuochan fayao (Xiao zhiguan), T46.1915.0471c, in Taishō shinshū daizōkyō vol. 46, Tokyo 1924-1934, read in the CBETA edition of that text", "Zhiyi, Xiuxi zhiguan zuochan fayao (Xiao zhiguan), T46.1915.0472a, in Taishō shinshū daizōkyō vol. 46, Tokyo 1924-1934, read in the CBETA edition of that text" .

pmc:TiantaiZhuKongLuoDi a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:XiaoZhiguan ;
    rdfs:label "Abiding in the first contemplation one falls to the ground of the hearers"@en, "Verweilt man in der ersten Schau, fällt man auf den Boden der Hörer"@de ;
    skos:definition "The failure the last chapter attaches to the first of its three contemplations: if one abides in this contemplation, one falls to the ground of the hearers and the solitary buddhas. The reason given is that the one who sees the unconditioned and enters the right position will in the end not be able to bring forth the awakening mind, this being an excess of the power of the absorption, by which the buddha nature is not seen. Of the second contemplation the same form is used with the other excess: abiding there, the power of wisdom is great, and so although the buddha nature is seen it is not seen clearly."@en ;
    skos:note "A failure condition of a stage and not of an operation, and it has the shape the work uses everywhere: the fault is an excess of one of the two halves, and the remedy is the other half. It is worth keeping because it is a failure that only shows in what the practitioner cannot do next, which is the hardest kind for an outsider to check and the easiest for a transmission to assert."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Tiantai ;
    dcterms:source "Zhiyi, Xiuxi zhiguan zuochan fayao (Xiao zhiguan), T46.1915.0472b, in Taishō shinshū daizōkyō vol. 46, Tokyo 1924-1934, read in the CBETA edition of that text" .

#
# The signs of success, kept separate and counted separately
#

pmc:TiantaiZhongzuiMiexiang a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:XiaoZhiguan ;
    rdfs:label "The marks by which the extinction of a grave offence is known"@en, "Die Merkmale, an denen das Erloschensein einer schweren Schuld erkannt wird"@de ;
    skos:definition "The work asks its own question, how is the mark of the extinction of a grave offence known, and answers with a list: if the practitioner repents with a mind thus set, he feels of himself that body and mind are light and quick, and gets good auspicious dreams; or he sees numinous and unusual signs; or he feels the good mind opening; or in the sitting he feels the body as a cloud or a shadow, and by this gradually attains and reaches the fields of the absorptions; or understanding suddenly arises, he knows the marks of the teachings well, and grasps the sense of whatever scripture he hears, and from this joy in the teaching his mind is without grief or regret."@en ;
    skos:note "A sign of success in the strict sense of SPEC §15, told to the practitioner in advance of the practice that is to produce it, and therefore counted apart from the conditions of failure in this file and in the harvest note. The first item is the state of the body as the practitioner himself feels it, the second is a dream, the third a vision, and none of the five is available to anybody else."@en ;
    skos:note "The term is worth noting beside it. The confession is to be carried for one or three weeks, a month, three months or up to a year, and it stops when extinction is reached. The stopping rule is the sign, so the term is not a term: a practitioner who has not yet seen the marks has not reached the end of the procedure, and there is no length of time after which the procedure is to be called finished without them."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Tiantai ;
    dcterms:source "Zhiyi, Xiuxi zhiguan zuochan fayao (Xiao zhiguan), T46.1915.0463a, in Taishō shinshū daizōkyō vol. 46, Tokyo 1924-1934, read in the CBETA edition of that text" .

pmc:TiantaiChanhuiYieldsMiexiang a pm:Yielding ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:XiaoZhiguan ;
    rdfs:label "The repentance procedure brings the marks of extinction and opens the absorption"@en, "Das Reueverfahren bringt die Merkmale des Erloschenseins und öffnet die Vertiefung"@de ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:TiantaiChanhui ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:TiantaiZhongzuiMiexiang ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Tiantai ;
    dcterms:source "Zhiyi, Xiuxi zhiguan zuochan fayao (Xiao zhiguan), T46.1915.0463a, in Taishō shinshū daizōkyō vol. 46, Tokyo 1924-1934, read in the CBETA edition of that text" .

pmc:TiantaiShangenFaxiang a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:XiaoZhiguan ;
    rdfs:label "The five kinds of sign by which the good roots are known to arise"@en, "Die fünf Arten von Zeichen, an denen das Aufgehen der guten Wurzeln erkannt wird"@de ;
    skos:definition "Five inward kinds, each with its own marks. In the breath kind, body and mind grow empty and still and the practitioner does not see the shape of either, and after one or two sittings or one or two days or one or two months, holding without losing or falling back, he suddenly feels body and mind move and the eight touches arise: pain, itch, cold, warmth, lightness, heaviness, roughness, smoothness, and while they arise body and mind are settled and the pleasure is beyond comparison; or he feels the breath long and short through all the pores and sees with the mind's eye the thirty-six things inside his own body as one sees beans in an opened granary. In the kind of the contemplation of the unclean he sees a corpse swell and rot, or his own bones joint by joint. In the kind of the loving mind he suddenly loves living beings and his face is constantly at peace to whomever he meets. In the kind of the contemplation of conditions understanding suddenly arises in him. In the kind of the recollection of the buddhas he remembers their merits, the body grows light and quick and he feels his own merit towering and loved by men."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted apart from the conditions of failure. Three of the five end in a state of the practitioner that only he reports; two end in something a bystander could notice, a face at peace and a body light and quick, and one, the seeing of the thirty-six things within the body, is a claim about a hidden matter of fact that could in principle be put against an anatomy. The work makes nothing of that and does not propose it as a check."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Tiantai ;
    dcterms:source "Zhiyi, Xiuxi zhiguan zuochan fayao (Xiao zhiguan), T46.1915.0469b to 0469c, in Taishō shinshū daizōkyō vol. 46, Tokyo 1924-1934, read in the CBETA edition of that text" .

pmc:TiantaiXieweiChan a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:XiaoZhiguan ;
    rdfs:label "The false and counterfeit absorption, with the marks that distinguish it from the true"@en, "Die falsche und nachgemachte Vertiefung, mit den Merkmalen, die sie von der echten scheiden"@de ;
    skos:definition "The work sets the two sides out one after the other. The marks of the false: the body itches or scratches, or is heavy as if pressed by a weight, or light as if about to fly, or as if bound, or sags and droops, or is parched with cold, or burning hot; or various strange realms are seen; or the mind is darkened; or evil thoughts arise; or thoughts scatter outward to miscellaneous good works; or there is joy and agitation; or grief and mourning; or an evil touch raises the hair of the body; or a great pleasure that stupefies. The marks of the true: none of the above occurs, and as each absorption arises the practitioner feels it answer to the settled state, empty and bright and pure, the inward mind glad, calmly happy, without covering; the good mind opens, faith and reverence increase, knowledge is clear, body and mind are soft, subtle and empty and still, the world is found wearisome, and there is no doing and no wanting, and the going in and the coming out are free. The work gives the criterion as a comparison: it is as with keeping company, where an evil man offends again and again and a good man is found fair the longer one sees him."@en ;
    skos:note "The counterfeit of its own sign of success, set beside the sign, with a list on each side and a criterion between them, and the list of the false is the longer of the two. This is the material the holding keeps in examinations/the-sign-and-the-counterfeit.ttl, and this transmission supplies it unasked and for its own procedure."@en ;
    skos:note "What the two lists do not do is divide the cases. Several of the false marks are bodily states that also stand in the list of the good roots at pmc:TiantaiShangenFaxiang: lightness, heaviness, cold, warmth, itch and pain are there called the eight touches and are a sign of the breath kind arising, and here they are the sign of a counterfeit. The work does not resolve it, and the criterion it does give, that the true absorption answers to the settled state and the false does not, is a judgement by the practitioner about his own condition. What the lists separate is therefore not available to anybody but him."@en ;
    skos:note "The consequence the work attaches to mistaking them is the third of its paths to madness: if a man loves and clings to this false absorption, he answers to the ninety-five ways of ghosts and spirits, and often loses his mind and becomes deranged; and the spirits, knowing that he clings to their way, add their strength, so that he brings forth false absorptions and false knowledge, eloquence and powers, and moves the world; the ignorant who see him say he has gained the fruit of the way and all believe and submit, while his own mind is upside down. Such a man at the end of life never meets a buddha and falls back into the way of ghosts and spirits, and if in his sitting he did much evil he falls into hell."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Tiantai ;
    dcterms:source "Zhiyi, Xiuxi zhiguan zuochan fayao (Xiao zhiguan), T46.1915.0470a, in Taishō shinshū daizōkyō vol. 46, Tokyo 1924-1934, read in the CBETA edition of that text" .

#
# The clauses that close the account
#

pmc:TiantaiZheZhang a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:XiaoZhiguan ;
    rdfs:label "Tell no one the benefit, and do not doubt before harm is shown"@en, "Nenne niemandem den Nutzen, und zweifle nicht, bevor Schaden erwiesen ist"@de ;
    skos:definition "The tenth of the ten things the work requires beside the healing in the sitting, and the sentence that follows it. What is the recognition of hindrances: it means that the benefit gained is not spoken of outwardly, and that where there is not yet harm no doubt and no disparagement is to arise. If one follows these ten things, then what is treated will certainly have effect and will not be in vain."@en ;
    skos:note "The one unconditional guarantee in the work, and it is made conditional on ten things of which the tenth forbids both the report of a success and the doubt of a failure. A practitioner who followed the ten could not have produced a counter-instance: a cure he may not mention, and a case that has not yet done him harm he may not doubt. The guarantee and the device that protects it are the same sentence, as at pmc:BonLasChad in the Bön file, and here the device is the more complete of the two because it closes both directions."@en ;
    skos:note "The seventh of the same ten removes the term as well: what is long practice, it means that if the use of it brings no benefit at once, the days and months are not to be counted, and it is to be practised constantly and not given up. This belongs beside the holding's statement of that shape in generalizations/no-failed-run-counts.ttl, generalizations/the-rule-against-reporting-the-absence.ttl and examinations/the-count-that-does-not-fall-due.ttl, and it is not entered as a witness of any of the three: the counter-search against the holding that SPEC §16 requires before a generalization is enlarged was not carried in this run."@en ;
    skos:note "The other eight are given here so that the ten can be counted and the two in question not read as the whole: faith that this method can cure; use, meaning constant use at the time; diligence, meaning to use it with undivided attention until a cure is got; abiding in the object, meaning a fine mind following the method thought by thought without another object; distinguishing the cause of the illness; skill in means, meaning that the breathing out and in and the directing of the mind be well accomplished and not miss what is fitting; long practice; and the knowledge of what to retain and what to drop, meaning to be diligent where there is benefit and to drop and adjust where there is harm; and the care that discerns and avoids discordant causes."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Tiantai ;
    dcterms:source "Zhiyi, Xiuxi zhiguan zuochan fayao (Xiao zhiguan), T46.1915.0472b, in Taishō shinshū daizōkyō vol. 46, Tokyo 1924-1934, read in the CBETA edition of that text" .

pmc:TiantaiFeiMoSuoWei a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:XiaoZhiguan ;
    rdfs:label "The harm is suffered through the practitioner's own want of wisdom and is not the demon's doing"@en, "Der Schaden wird durch die eigene Unwissenheit des Übenden erlitten und ist nicht das Werk des Dämons"@de ;
    skos:definition "The work's own correction of the chapter it has just written. If the demonic realms do not withdraw, no grief is to arise, and if they are seen to withdraw, no joy either. For what reason: it has never yet been seen that a man in sitting saw a demon turn into a tiger or a wolf and come to eat him, nor has it ever been seen that a demon turned into a man or a woman and became his wife or husband. When they work their illusions the foolish man does not understand, his mind gives rise to fright or to clinging, and through this his mind is disordered, he loses the absorption and becomes deranged and brings the trouble on himself. All of this is the practitioner suffering through want of wisdom, and it is not what the demon did."@en ;
    skos:note "A transmission that has just given a bestiary of interferences by the hour of the day then says that none of them has ever been seen to do anything, and lays the whole of the damage on the practitioner. It is the most complete displacement of a failure in this holding so far, and it is more complete than the ones already entered because it does not merely move the blame but denies that the other party acts at all. The holding's statement of the shape is at generalizations/the-excuse-that-stops-at-the-officiant.ttl and generalizations/displaced-penalties.ttl; neither is enlarged by this run, for the reason given at pmc:TiantaiZheZhang."@en ;
    skos:note "The same passage is also the strongest negative report in the work, and it is a negative report about a class of events, which SPEC §13 counts the expensive kind: it has never been seen, said of a tradition's own demonology by the man who wrote its manual. What it rests on is not stated, and the work does not say who looked."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Tiantai ;
    dcterms:source "Zhiyi, Xiuxi zhiguan zuochan fayao (Xiao zhiguan), T46.1915.0471a, in Taishō shinshū daizōkyō vol. 46, Tokyo 1924-1934, read in the CBETA edition of that text" .

pmc:TiantaiJinshiBuchengjiu a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:XiaoZhiguan ;
    rdfs:label "People of the present time mostly do not accomplish these visualizations"@en, "Die Menschen der Gegenwart bringen diese Vorstellungsübungen meist nicht zustande"@de ;
    skos:definition "The withdrawal the work makes immediately after reporting the twelve breaths, the six sounded breaths and the borrowed visualizations: but the capacities of the men of the present time are shallow and dull, and making these visualizations they mostly do not accomplish them, and it is not transmitted in the world. The sentence goes on to forbid the obvious substitute: nor is one to go on from this to learn breath arts and the giving up of grain, for fear of giving rise to deviant views. Mineral and herbal medicines, where they answer to the illness, may be taken."@en ;
    skos:note "The reason the twelve prescriptions are entered as one claim and not as twelve, and the reason that decision is defensible. The work reports them, declines to own them, says they mostly fail now, and says that the failure is in the men and not in the method. That last move is the same one as at pmc:TiantaiFeiMoSuoWei, applied to a technique instead of to a demon, and it is what keeps a set of prescriptions in a manual after the manual has stopped expecting them to work."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Tiantai ;
    dcterms:source "Zhiyi, Xiuxi zhiguan zuochan fayao (Xiao zhiguan), T46.1915.0472a, in Taishō shinshū daizōkyō vol. 46, Tokyo 1924-1934, read in the CBETA edition of that text" .

pmc:TiantaiWuGai a pm:Presupposing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:XiaoZhiguan ;
    rdfs:label "Doubting himself, the practitioner will in the end not bring the absorption forth"@en, "Zweifelt er an sich, wird der Übende die Vertiefung am Ende nicht hervorbringen"@de ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:TiantaiZhiguan ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:TiantaiWuGaiList ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "Of the fifth covering, doubt, in its three kinds. Doubting himself: he thinks, my faculties are dark and dull, my guilt and defilement deep and heavy, am I the man for this. Making this doubt of himself, the dharma of the absorption will in the end not be brought forth; so if he would cultivate the absorption he must not make light of himself, since the roots of good of a former life are hard to measure. Doubting the teacher: he thinks, this man's bearing and appearance are so and so, he has no way in himself, how should he teach me. Making this doubt and contempt, that is an obstruction to the absorption. Doubting the dharma: men mostly hold to their own minds and cannot at once believe and receive with a reverent mind what is given them, and if the mind gives rise to hesitation, the dharma does not dye the mind. And of the first covering: a man of craving is very far from the way, for where the mind clings to craving there is no way to come near the way."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Tiantai ;
    skos:note "Found on the second pass over the work and not on the first, which is the reason the second pass was made. The clause is the sharpest prerequisite in the work after the exit procedure and it is of a different kind: what must be removed is not an act or a condition of life but the practitioner's own estimate of himself, and the consequence is that the thing he is practising for will never arise. A transmission that makes a man's doubt of his own fitness the reason his practice fails has put the one thing he cannot report truthfully in the position of the cause."@en ;
    skos:note "It is also the fourth of the work's devices, though it was not written as one. Doubt of the teaching keeps the teaching from dyeing the mind, doubt of the teacher obstructs the absorption, doubt of oneself keeps the absorption from arising at all; so a practitioner who sat and got nothing is told in advance that his doubt is a sufficient explanation. Beside the prohibition on reporting a benefit at pmc:TiantaiZheZhang and the clause at pmc:TiantaiFeiMoSuoWei that nothing but his own want of wisdom harmed him, this completes the account: the method cannot be answered because every outcome is about him."@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zhiyi, Xiuxi zhiguan zuochan fayao (Xiao zhiguan), T46.1915.0465a, in Taishō shinshū daizōkyō vol. 46, Tokyo 1924-1934, read in the CBETA edition of that text" .

pmc:TiantaiWuGaiList a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:XiaoZhiguan ;
    rdfs:label "The five coverings to be discarded, and why these five and not more"@en, "Die fünf zu verwerfenden Deckel, und warum diese fünf und nicht mehr"@de ;
    skos:definition "The third chapter. Five states are to be discarded as coverings over the good mind: craving, which arises here in the inner faculty and not in the outer objects; anger, which the work calls the root of losing the dharma and the great thief of the good mind; sleep, which is singled out as the worst because the other coverings can be removed once they are noticed, while sleep is like death and has no noticing in it, so that it is hard to remove, and against which the meditation weight and staff are prescribed where it lies heavy; agitation in body, mouth and mind, which becomes a covering only when regret follows it, since while a man is agitated he is not yet at his object; and doubt. The work puts its own objection and answers it: asked why, since bad states are numberless, only five are discarded, it answers that these five contain the three poisons and their equal share, that the four are the root, and that eight thousand four hundred vexations are gathered under them, so that discarding these five is discarding all that is not good."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered on the second pass. Two things in it are worth keeping apart from the prerequisite that stands on it. The work ranks its own five by how hard each is to remove and gives the criterion, which is whether the state can be noticed while it holds; and it answers the question why five, with an arithmetic. A list that justifies its own length is rarer in this holding than a list."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Tiantai ;
    dcterms:source "Zhiyi, Xiuxi zhiguan zuochan fayao (Xiao zhiguan), T46.1915.0464a to 0465b, in Taishō shinshū daizōkyō vol. 46, Tokyo 1924-1934, read in the CBETA edition of that text" .

pmc:TiantaiKongWuSuoHuo a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:XiaoZhiguan ;
    rdfs:label "Even if one gains nothing at all, one never turns back"@en, "Auch wenn man gar nichts erlangt, kehrt man nicht um"@de ;
    skos:definition "The fifth of the five expedients, single-mindedness, defined by what no outcome may change. Seeing clearly that the world is to be feared and hated, and knowing well that the merit of the absorption and the wisdom is to be honoured and held dear, one is then to cultivate calming and contemplation single-mindedly and with decision, with a mind like adamant, so that the demons of heaven and the outer ways cannot ruin it; and supposing it be empty and one gains nothing at all, one will in the end not turn back and change. That is called single-mindedness."@en ;
    skos:note "Found on the second pass. It is the strongest of the devices in the work, and unlike the clause at pmc:TiantaiZheZhang it protects no particular promise: it is written into the definition of a required disposition that the absence of any result whatever is not to change the practice. A practitioner who has it cannot stop for a negative outcome, because stopping for one would mean he never had the fifth expedient and so was never doing the thing properly."@en ;
    skos:note "What keeps it from being merely an exhortation is where it stands. The five expedients are the fifth chapter of ten and are required before the main practice begins; so the work states the condition that no outcome counts before it has stated a single outcome. Beside it, the sentence two chapters later that there is nothing the gathered mind does not accomplish, at pmc:TiantaiZhixinYichu, reads differently: the guarantee is made to a practitioner who has already agreed that its failure would prove nothing."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Tiantai ;
    dcterms:source "Zhiyi, Xiuxi zhiguan zuochan fayao (Xiao zhiguan), T46.1915.0466c, in Taishō shinshū daizōkyō vol. 46, Tokyo 1924-1934, read in the CBETA edition of that text" .

pmp:TiantaiJingmei a pm:Practicing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:XiaoZhiguan ;
    rdfs:label "Identifying the sprite by the double-hour at which it comes, and naming it"@en, "Den Kobold an der Doppelstunde seines Kommens erkennen und ihn benennen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The first of the three kinds of interference and the only one of them with a procedure. The twelve beasts of the twelve double-hours change into shapes and colours of every kind, a young girl, an old man, a shape to be feared, to vex and confuse the practitioner, and each comes at its own hour, so that the hour tells which it is: at the third double-hour a tiger, at the fourth a hare or deer, at the fifth a dragon or turtle, at the sixth a snake, at the seventh a horse, ass or camel, at the eighth a sheep, at the ninth an ape, at the tenth a cock or crow, at the eleventh a dog or wolf, at the twelfth a pig, at the first a rat, at the second an ox. If the practitioner sees that it constantly comes at this hour, he knows it for that beast's sprite, and on his speaking its name and reproaching it, it withdraws at once."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered on the second pass, and it belongs in the file because it is the one place in the work where a claim is made with an antecedent a second person in the room could check. The hour is on the clock, the identification is a prediction from the hour, and the stated effect of naming the beast is that the interference ends at once. The work says in the same chapter, at pmc:TiantaiFeiMoSuoWei, that no such interference has ever been seen to do anything; the two stand four pages apart and the work does not reconcile them."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Tiantai ;
    dcterms:source "Zhiyi, Xiuxi zhiguan zuochan fayao (Xiao zhiguan), T46.1915.0470b, in Taishō shinshū daizōkyō vol. 46, Tokyo 1924-1934, read in the CBETA edition of that text" .

pmc:TiantaiJingmeiXiemie a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:XiaoZhiguan ;
    rdfs:label "Named and reproached, the sprite withdraws at once"@en, "Benannt und gescholten, weicht der Kobold auf der Stelle"@de ;
    skos:definition "The capacity claimed for the naming: if the practitioner sees that the thing constantly comes at this double-hour, he knows it for that beast's sprite, and on his speaking its name and reproaching it, it withdraws at once. The second kind of interference is met by the same means with a fixed form of words: eyes shut and single-minded, the practitioner curses it inwardly and says, I know you now, you are such and such a kind in this world, a stealer of fire and smeller of incense, of false views, glad to break the precepts; I keep the precepts and in the end do not fear you; and a monk is to recite the precept text and a layman the three refuges and five precepts, whereupon the thing goes off backwards, crawling."@en ;
    skos:note "A misfiring condition is stated with it and is the reason this is the most answerable claim in the chapter: the procedure is to be applied when the thing comes constantly at one hour, so a thing that comes at no fixed hour is not to be met this way. The work therefore names a case in which its own identification does not apply, which is the shape of a condition of failure, in a chapter it afterwards says describes nothing that happens."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Tiantai ;
    dcterms:source "Zhiyi, Xiuxi zhiguan zuochan fayao (Xiao zhiguan), T46.1915.0470b, in Taishō shinshū daizōkyō vol. 46, Tokyo 1924-1934, read in the CBETA edition of that text" .

pmc:TiantaiJingmeiYieldsXiemie a pm:Yielding ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:XiaoZhiguan ;
    rdfs:label "Naming the sprite makes the interference cease"@en, "Die Benennung des Kobolds bringt die Störung zum Erliegen"@de ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:TiantaiJingmei ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:TiantaiJingmeiXiemie ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Tiantai ;
    dcterms:source "Zhiyi, Xiuxi zhiguan zuochan fayao (Xiao zhiguan), T46.1915.0470b, in Taishō shinshū daizōkyō vol. 46, Tokyo 1924-1934, read in the CBETA edition of that text" .

pmc:TiantaiZhixinYichu a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:XiaoZhiguan ;
    rdfs:label "Hold the mind in one place and there is nothing that is not accomplished"@en, "Halte den Geist an einem Ort, und nichts bleibt unvollbracht"@de ;
    skos:definition "The quotation with which the work closes the chapter on the arising of the good roots, after listing the five kinds and adding that the whole range of the gates of the teaching may arise in the same way: therefore the scripture says, hold the mind in one place and there is nothing that is not accomplished."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered because it is the counter-weight to everything in this file and stands in the same work, two chapters before the healing chapter puts the four hundred and four illnesses on both sides of the same operation. The two are not in contradiction, and that is the finding: what this transmission holds open to failure is the performance, and what it holds closed is the method."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Tiantai ;
    dcterms:source "Zhiyi, Xiuxi zhiguan zuochan fayao (Xiao zhiguan), T46.1915.0469c, in Taishō shinshū daizōkyō vol. 46, Tokyo 1924-1934, read in the CBETA edition of that text" .
